@@s001 Topological sort
@@s002 ora:node written now,dato:in the list (degree 0),no:degree still > 0,ok:already written
@@s003 DAG: every arrow goes "forward". I ask for an order of all the nodes in which every arrow goes from left to right. I count the <b>incoming edges</b> (in) of each node.
@@s004 Incoming degree 0: <b>a</b> and <b>b</b>. No arrow enters them: they can go first. I put them in the list.
@@s005 I write <b>a</b> and remove it from the graph. Its outgoing arrows (towards c and e) disappear: c drops to 1, e drops to 0.
@@s006 I write <b>b</b>. Now c drops to 0. The list contains e and c, both with degree 0.
@@s007 I write <b>e</b>: d drops to 1. I write <b>c</b>: d drops to 0.
@@s008 I write <b>d</b>. Final order: a, b, e, c, d. Every edge goes from left to right: that is why it exists. If at some point the list were empty with nodes still remaining, there is a cycle and an order does not exist.
@@s009 Topological sort
@@s010 A topological sort puts the nodes of a DAG in a row, so that every edge goes from left to right. It is needed every time there are dependencies: prerequisites, orders of computation, chains of decisions.
@@s011 The problem and the DAG
@@s012 A <b>DAG</b> (directed acyclic graph) is a directed graph without cycles. A topological order is a sequence of all the nodes such that every edge <code>u → v</code> has <code>u</code> before <code>v</code>. It exists <b>if and only if</b> the graph is a DAG.
@@s013 Example: courses with their prerequisites. A course can be followed only after its prerequisites, so the legal sequence is a topological order.
@@s014 The method with the incoming degrees
@@s015 The incoming degree (in-degree) of a node is the number of edges that enter it. Kahn's method:
@@s016 Compute the incoming degree of each node.
@@s017 Put in a list the nodes with degree 0 (the <b>sources</b>).
@@s018 Take one, write it in the order, and decrease by 1 the degree of its successors. Whoever reaches 0 enters the list.
@@s019 Repeat until the list is empty.
@@s020 Why it works: in a DAG there is always at least one source, and removing it leaves a DAG. Cost <code>O(n + m)</code>.
@@s021 Kahn's method: first the sources
@@s022 The method with the DFS
@@s023 Alternative: do a DFS on the whole graph, and write each node when it <b>finishes</b> (when all its neighbours have been visited). Then read the list of finishes <b>backwards</b>: it is a topological order. If the DFS finds a back edge, the graph has a cycle.
@@s024 Finish in reverse
@@s025 The node that finishes last, in a DAG, is a source: no arc enters it from the rest of the graph that is not already finished. That is why it goes first in the reversed order.
@@s026 When it exists
@@s027 A topological order exists only in a DAG. If the graph has a cycle, no order can make all the arrows go from left to right: the nodes of the cycle would chase each other. Kahn's method notices it: at the end nodes remain unwritten.
@@s028 The code, side by side
@@s029 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
